
  My self-review did find two real gaps, which I fixed in the spec:

  1. [1m[38;5;153mconfig.mjs[39m had no stated values.[0m An implementer would have invented them. Now pinned: [38;5;153mLOGIN_URL[39m preserves the
     existing [38;5;153mhttps://api.example.com/login[39m verbatim (and the orphaned [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m gets removed),
     [38;5;153mANALYTICS_URL[39m is a new placeholder.
  2. [1mThe manual-verification claim was misleading.[0m Section 4 said the DOM wiring would be "verified by hand in the
     browser," but neither placeholder URL resolves — so a real submit always takes the network-error path. Hand
     verification can confirm the wiring runs and reports a failure; it cannot show a successful login or a tracking
     POST. The spec now says so, and the Testing section no longer implies end-to-end confidence.

  ---

  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-login-userid-tracking-design.md[39m (untracked, not committed). Please
  review it and let me know if you want changes before I write the implementation plan.

  Two things in it I'd particularly like your eye on, since nothing external has checked them:

  - [1mThe contracts in Section 2 are invented.[0m I made up the login response shape and the analytics payload against
    servers that don't exist. If you have any idea what the real ones will look like, correcting them now is free;
    correcting them after implementation is not.
  - [1m[38;5;153mlogin[39m dropping the [38;5;153muser[39m field and becoming async[0m is the one change that would break an outside caller. Nothing in
    this repo calls it besides the submit handler, but if anything outside this repo does, I don't know about it.

